+2.1 If $y\in N\setminus G\cdot e$, conjugate it into $\mathfrak n^+$ by [F1]. At least one simple-root coefficient must vanish by [F2]. For each simple $\alpha_i$, set $\mathfrak n_i=\bigoplus_{\beta\in\Phi^+,\,\beta\ne\alpha_i}\mathfrak g_\beta$. This is a nilpotent Lie subalgebra. It is an ideal in $\mathfrak p_{\alpha_i}=\mathfrak b\oplus\mathfrak g_{-\alpha_i}$: brackets with $\mathfrak h$ preserve each root space; brackets with positive root spaces have weights $\beta+\gamma$, which if nonzero remain positive and cannot equal $\alpha_i$ because $\beta\ne\alpha_i$ has a positive coefficient at another simple root; and if $\beta-\alpha_i$ is a root for $\beta\ne\alpha_i$, it must be positive, since otherwise $\alpha_i$ would be a sum of positive roots. These assertions use [F5] and the simple-root basis. The exponential/BCH coordinates in [F5] identify $\exp:\mathfrak n^+\to U$ as a polynomial isomorphism, so $N_i=\exp(\mathfrak n_i)$ is a closed connected unipotent subgroup with Lie algebra $\mathfrak n_i$. Order the positive-root coordinates with $\alpha_i$ first. The product of the remaining root subgroups is then a closed subvariety of $U$ of dimension $|\Phi^+|-1$; it lies in $N_i$, which has the same dimension, so equals $N_i$. Hence $U=U_{\alpha_i}N_i$. Since $\mathfrak n_i$ is an ideal, its normalizer in the connected group $P_{\alpha_i}$ has full Lie algebra and hence equals $P_{\alpha_i}$; thus $N_i$ is normal in $P_{\alpha_i}$. The quotient $P_{\alpha_i}/N_i$ is generated by $T$ and the images of $U_{\alpha_i}$ and $U_{-\alpha_i}$. The rank-one homomorphism of [[lem-semisimple-rank-one-sl2-root-homomorphism]] supplies the semisimple rank-one subgroup. The central subtorus $(\ker\alpha_i)^0\subseteq T$ centralizes it, and $\alpha_i\circ\alpha_i^\vee(t)=t^2$ shows that its quotient in $T$ is covered by the coroot torus up to finite index. Hence $P_{\alpha_i}/N_i$ is a central product of a torus and a semisimple rank-one group, so it is reductive and has